 that's merely apparent of  course if it is something that appears  it could also be real it could be  something that does take place and is  noted as appearing precisely because  there's something substantive some  substantively there but the sliding  let's talk about that ologurian so this  is uh coming from the greek word for  less or few right we get uh oligarchy  from that rule by a few who tend to be  the rich right oligoria on sliding  you're reducing  somebody to  something that they don't want to have  going on and aristotle actually as we'll  talk about elsewhere  defines sliding and then characterizes  it in three different main modes and  then goes on to tell us quite a lot  about what counts as  sliding so let's just put that to the  side for a moment the sliding has a  couple important characteristics  that help to lead to the anger response  sliding isn't just against  anybody or anything in general  it's personal  it's connected with the person who gets  angry so the sliding is either against  self ace how tone right  that's the sort of reflexive  pronoun it's against me it's against you  it's against the other person who gets  angry i'm being slighted  or  it's against those one is concerned with  some translations will say things like  one's friends or one's family  but really the greek is  quite generic here anybody  who you  care about anybody literally who is of  you maybe you identify with them maybe  you think they're cool maybe you feel  bad for them and you try to take care of  them  maybe it's not even a person maybe it's  an inanimate object or a geographical  feature  or some sort of abstraction aristotle  will talk about people getting angry  when other people make fun of the things  that they  care about like philosophy  right so  you take the slight as being either  against you or against  anything anyone  that you care about you're walking down  the street and somebody says holy crap  is that an ugly dog that you're walking  it's your